## Solution

**Problem:** Determine the number of zeros of $p(z) = z^7 + z(z-3)^3 + 1$ in the open disk $|z-3|<1$.

**Answer-leak check:** The problem statement contains only the polynomial and the question — no solution, hint, or formalization note is present. No leak detected.

---

### Proof

We apply **Rouché's theorem** on the circle $|z-3|=1$ (the boundary of the disk). Write

$$p(z) = f(z) + g(z), \qquad f(z) := z^7, \qquad g(z) := z(z-3)^3 + 1.$$

**Bounding $|f(z)|$ on $|z-3|=1$.** For any $z$ with $|z-3|=1$, the triangle inequality gives

$$|z| = |(z-3)+3| \geq |3|-|z-3| = 3-1 = 2.$$

Hence

$$|f(z)| = |z|^7 \geq 2^7 = 128. \tag{1}$$

**Bounding $|g(z)|$ on $|z-3|=1$.** Using $|z-3|=1$ and $|z|\leq |z-3|+3 = 4$,

$$|g(z)| = |z(z-3)^3 + 1| \leq |z|\,|z-3|^3 + 1 = |z|\cdot 1 + 1 \leq 4 + 1 = 5. \tag{2}$$

**Comparison.** From (1) and (2),

$$|g(z)| \leq 5 < 128 \leq |f(z)| \quad \text{for all } z \text{ with } |z-3|=1.$$

The inequality is strict everywhere on the boundary, so Rouché's theorem applies: $p(z) = f(z)+g(z)$ has the same number of zeros (counting multiplicities) inside $|z-3|<1$ as $f(z) = z^7$.

**Counting zeros of $f(z)=z^7$ inside $|z-3|<1$.** The only zero of $z^7$ is at $z=0$ (multiplicity 7). Its distance from the center $z=3$ is

$$|0 - 3| = 3 \not< 1.$$

So $z=0$ lies **outside** the disk $|z-3|<1$, and $z^7$ has **zero** zeros inside the disk.

**Conclusion.** By Rouché's theorem, $p(z)$ has the same number of zeros inside $|z-3|<1$ as $z^7$, namely

$$\boxed{0}.$$

